{
  "family": "limitsApply",
  "description": "Apply-time WireLimits (WIRE_FORMAT section 21): every op that can grow a tree is checked on its RESULT, and one that takes the tree past MaxDepth (24) or MaxNodes (100000) is refused with the shared code LimitExceeded. The decoder bounds what arrives; these vectors pin that an op assembled from legal parts cannot build what no host could decode. Each vector carries `input.tree` (a wire node) and `input.op` (a wire op) as canonical JSON STRINGS, decoded by the host's own wire decoder. `expected.verdict` is `reject` or `accept`. A `reject` vector requires apply to refuse with `expected.code`; an `accept` vector requires it to apply (no result bytes are pinned: the family is about the limit, and every accepted shape here is already pinned by the node and op corpora). The ops covered are the three the earlier guard missed - EditNode and UpdateState, which put subtrees in, and MoveNode, which can stack two legal depths - plus the repeated-id shape that defeats an id-set derivation. InsertChild and ReplaceRoot are pinned by each host's own suite. MaxNodes is NOT pinned here, deliberately: a vector past it is a 100001-node document, several megabytes of corpus to restate one comparison each host already unit-tests on the same ops.",
  "limits": {
    "maxDepth": 24,
    "maxNodes": 100000
  },
  "vectors": [
    {
